+# Formal isogeny series and invariant differentials
+
+The formal series of an isogeny is obtained from the Laurent expansion of its
+pullback of the local parameter `t = −x/y` at the identity. For a pullback that
+fixes the identity, this parameter has positive order and the resulting power
+series has zero constant term.
+
+The coefficient identities relate this series to the scalar by which the
+pullback acts on the invariant differential. They are expressed using explicit
+constant and coefficient witnesses. Substitution in the formal group law gives
+additivity of the linear coefficient and induction for multiplication by an
+integer.
+
+The pullback and point map in the abstract isogeny structure are independent.
+Consequently, order and coefficient hypotheses must concern the pullback;
+relations between point maps alone do not imply the corresponding identities.
+
+The formal Laurent valuation agrees with the order at infinity, by comparison
+on the `F(X)`-basis `1, y`. The pole arguments retain their explicit basepoint
+hypotheses.
+
+References: Silverman, *The Arithmetic of Elliptic Curves*, IV.1, IV.2, IV.4.
