+# Differential coefficients and the formal group
+
+For nonzero integers `m`, the multiplication series has linear coefficient `m`
+(Silverman IV.2.3a). Its proof uses the chord/tangent addition identity and
+negation. The zero multiplication datum has identity pullback, so its formal
+series is `X`; the nonzero hypothesis is essential for this formalization.
+
+The local expansion map transports Kähler differentials to Laurent series.
+The invariant differential has constant coefficient one, and the chain rule
+identifies a constant pullback coefficient with the linear coefficient of the
+formal isogeny series when the pulled-back x-coordinate has a pole at infinity.
+Concrete constant coefficients are proved for identity, integer multiplication,
+Frobenius and `1 + Frobenius`, with composition and addition formulas.
+
+In positive characteristic, the differential kernel is the field of p-th
+powers: the function field has degree `p` over its Frobenius image, while the
+kernel is a proper intermediate field. This gives the p-th-power property for
+the multiplication pullback.
+
+References: Silverman, *The Arithmetic of Elliptic Curves*, III.5 and IV.1–4.
 -/
 
